# 004 · Scan the history for secrets

**Status:** `IN_PROGRESS` · **Version:** [v0.1.0](../roadmap.md#v010) · **Part:** Tester basics

## What

Run a secret scanner (gitleaks) over every branch that will be published, and add the scan to CI. Allowlist the fake, key-shaped values that the tests use.

## Why

The history was reviewed with a denylist and a term scan before it was published. No secret scanner has run on it yet.

## Done when

- [x] A scan of every published branch finds only allowlisted test values.
- [ ] CI runs the scan on every push.

## Decisions

None yet.

## Open questions

None.

## Progress

- 2026-10-19: Goal written from the v0.1.0 release plan.
- 2026-10-20: CI has a **secret scan** job. It downloads the gitleaks 8.30.1 release archive, checks it against the SHA-256 in the release's published checksums file, and scans the whole history of the commit under test (`--log-opts="--full-history HEAD"`), with the values redacted. It uses the release archive and not the gitleaks GitHub Action, because the action needs a license key for repositories that an organization owns.
- 2026-10-20: `.gitleaks.toml` keeps gitleaks' default rules and adds three allowlists. Each one names a rule, a file and an exact value, and a finding is skipped only when all three match:
  - the made-up RunPod key in the two RunPod test files of control;
  - a made-up model route key in an older version of the agents acceptance script;
  - the two armour constants of the deploy-key maker, with nothing between them.

  A check against made-up files showed that the allowlists stay narrow: another value in an allowlisted file, the same value in another file, and a body between the armour lines are all still found.
- 2026-10-20: The scan over the published branches. Tool: gitleaks 8.30.1 from its official image, `ghcr.io/gitleaks/gitleaks:v8.30.1`. Scope: the whole history of `v1/build` (at `4a44d07`) and `main` (at `497c1a9`), 269 commits with changes. With the default rules only, it found 4 values. Each one was read at its commit: the same made-up RunPod key in two test files, the made-up route key, and the armour constants with no key between them. None is a secret. With `.gitleaks.toml`, it finds none. The CI box waits for the first push that runs the job.

